Title: THE TELL-TALE HEART
Credit: Written by
Author: Edgar Allan Poe
Source: Adapted from the short story by Edgar Allan Poe
Draft date: 7/31/2026

FADE IN:

INT. ASYLUM CELL - NIGHT

A sparse stone cell lit by a single flickering lantern. THE NARRATOR, a gaunt man in his thirties with wild unkempt black hair, pale skin and intense eyes, sits facing us in a wooden chair, hands gripping the arms.

(SOUND: distant echoing water drip, low wind outside the bars)

NARRATOR
True! -- nervous -- very, very dreadfully nervous I had been and am; but why will you say that I am mad? The disease had sharpened my senses -- not destroyed -- not dulled them. Above all was the sense of hearing acute. I heard all things in the heaven and in the earth. I heard many things in hell. How, then, am I mad? Hearken! and observe how healthily -- how calmly I can tell you the whole story.

(MUSIC: low tense strings, barely audible)

NARRATOR
It is impossible to say how first the idea entered my brain; but once conceived, it haunted me day and night. Object there was none. Passion there was none. I loved the old man. He had never wronged me. He had never given me insult. For his gold I had no desire. I think it was his eye! Yes, it was this! He had the eye of a vulture -- a pale blue eye, with a film over it. Whenever it fell upon me, my blood ran cold; and so by degrees -- very gradually -- I made up my mind to take the life of the old man, and thus rid myself of the eye forever.

(SOUND: faint, irregular heartbeat pulse under the music)

INT. OLD MAN'S BEDROOM - NIGHT

Thick darkness fills the shuttered room. An OLD MAN, frail with white hair and a clouded pale blue eye, lies asleep in a heavy wooden bed. The door latch turns with agonizing slowness. THE NARRATOR's head eases through the narrow gap, eyes fixed on the bed.

(SOUND: soft metallic click of the latch, floorboard creak)

NARRATOR (V.O.)
Now this is the point. You fancy me mad. Madmen know nothing. But you should have seen me. You should have seen how wisely I proceeded -- with what caution -- with what foresight -- with what dissimulation I went to work! I was never kinder to the old man than during the whole week before I killed him.

The dark lantern in THE NARRATOR's hand opens a hair's width. A single thin ray strikes the OLD MAN's closed eyelid.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
And every night, about midnight, I turned the latch of his door and opened it -- oh, so gently! And then, when I had made an opening sufficient for my head, I put in a dark lantern, all closed, closed, that no light shone out, and then I thrust in my head. Oh, you would have laughed to see how cunningly I thrust it in! I moved it slowly -- very, very slowly, so that I might not disturb the old man's sleep. It took me an hour to place my whole head within the opening so far that I could see him as he lay upon his bed. Ha! -- would a madman have been so wise as this?

(MUSIC: single sustained violin note, uneasy)

The ray finds only the closed eye again. THE NARRATOR withdraws as silently as he came. Dawn light begins to edge the shutters.

INT. OLD MAN'S BEDROOM - DAY

Morning sun cuts across the room. THE NARRATOR enters boldly, smiling, carrying a breakfast tray. The OLD MAN sits up, eye filmed and dull.

NARRATOR
(in hearty tone)
Good morning. How have you passed the night?

The OLD MAN answers with a weak nod. THE NARRATOR chats a moment longer, then leaves. The OLD MAN settles back, unsuspecting.

(SOUND: cheerful morning birds outside, undercut by a single low heartbeat thump)

INT. OLD MAN'S BEDROOM - NIGHT

Midnight again. The room is black as pitch, shutters fastened tight. THE NARRATOR eases the door open more slowly than ever -- slower than a watch's minute hand. His face shows barely contained triumph.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
Upon the eighth night I was more than usually cautious in opening the door. A watch's minute hand moves more quickly than did mine. Never before that night had I felt the extent of my own powers -- of my sagacity. I could scarcely contain my feelings of triumph. To think that there I was, opening the door, little by little, and he not even to dream of my secret deeds or thoughts. I fairly chuckled at the idea.

The OLD MAN stirs suddenly on the bed.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
And perhaps he heard me; for he moved on the bed suddenly, as if startled. Now you may think that I drew back -- but no. His room was as black as pitch with the thick darkness, and so I knew that he could not see the opening of the door, and I kept pushing it on steadily, steadily.

(SOUND: fabric rustle of the OLD MAN sitting up, then dead silence)

THE NARRATOR freezes, head inside the room, lantern unopened. A full hour passes without motion. The OLD MAN remains upright, listening.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
Presently I heard a slight groan, and I knew it was the groan of mortal terror. It was not a groan of pain or of grief -- oh, no! -- it was the low stifled sound that arises from the bottom of the soul when overcharged with awe. I knew the sound well. Many a night, just at midnight, when all the world slept, it has welled up from my own bosom. I knew what the old man felt, and pitied him, although I chuckled at heart.

A new, faint sound begins beneath the silence -- low, dull, quick -- like a watch enveloped in cotton.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
And now at the dead hour of the night, amid the dreadful silence of that old house, so strange a noise as this excited me to uncontrollable terror. Yet, for some minutes longer I refrained and stood still. But the beating grew louder, louder! I thought the heart must burst.

(MUSIC: heartbeat rhythm rises, matching the described sound, low and muffled)

THE NARRATOR's thumb slips on the lantern fastening. The OLD MAN bolts upright.

OLD MAN
Who's there?

Absolute stillness. THE NARRATOR does not breathe. The heartbeat sound grows steadily louder, quicker.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
It was the beating of the old man's heart. It increased my fury, as the beating of a drum stimulates the soldier into courage. The old man's terror must have been extreme! It grew louder, I say, louder every moment! -- do you mark me well?

The single ray of light shoots out, thin as a spider's thread, and lands directly on the open vulture eye -- wide, wide open, dull blue with the hideous film.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
It was open -- wide, wide open -- and I grew furious as I gazed upon it. I saw it with perfect distinctness -- all a dull blue, with a hideous veil over it that chilled the very marrow in my bones.

The heartbeat thunders now, filling the room. THE NARRATOR can bear it no longer.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
The old man's hour had come! With a loud yell, I threw open the lantern and leaped into the room.

(SOUND: sharp yell, wood splintering, heavy bed dragged across floorboards)

THE NARRATOR drags the OLD MAN to the floor, yanks the heavy bed over him. The muffled heartbeat continues beneath it for many minutes, then slows... and ceases.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
At length it ceased. The old man was dead. I removed the bed and examined the corpse. Yes, he was stone, stone dead. His eye would trouble me no more.

(SOUND: final heartbeat thump fading into silence, then a clock striking four)

INT. OLD MAN'S BEDROOM - NIGHT

Four o'clock. Still dark as midnight. THE NARRATOR works with silent haste. He dismembers the corpse with quick, precise cuts, catching every drop in a wooden tub. Three floor planks are lifted. The pieces are deposited between the scantlings. The boards are replaced so perfectly that no seam shows.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
First of all I dismembered the corpse. I cut off the head and the arms and the legs. I then took up three planks from the flooring of the chamber, and deposited all between the scantlings. I then replaced the boards so cleverly, so cunningly, that no human eye -- not even his -- could have detected anything wrong. There was nothing to wash out -- no stain of any kind -- no blood-spot whatever. A tub had caught all -- ha! ha!

(SOUND: soft scrape of planks being replaced, tub set aside)

A loud knocking sounds from the street door below.

INT. OLD MAN'S HOUSE - NIGHT

THE NARRATOR, calm and light-hearted, opens the front door with a lantern. Three men stand outside: OFFICER GROVES, OFFICER MASON and OFFICER FINN, all in dark uniforms.

OFFICER GROVES
We received information of a shriek heard during the night. Suspicion of foul play. We are here to search the premises.

NARRATOR
(smiling)
The shriek was my own in a dream. The old man is absent in the country. Please, search well.

He leads them through the house, then directly into the bedroom. He shows them the undisturbed treasures. With wild audacity he brings three chairs and sets them in the exact center of the room, directly over the hidden corpse. He sits upon his own chair atop the planks.

NARRATOR
Rest here, gentlemen, from your fatigues.

The officers sit. They chat of familiar things. THE NARRATOR answers cheerily at first. Then his face grows pale. A low, dull, quick sound begins in the room -- the same cotton-wrapped watch sound.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
I felt myself getting pale and wished them gone. My head ached, and I fancied a ringing in my ears... but it continued and gained definiteness -- until, at length, I found that the noise was not within my ears.

(SOUND: heartbeat sound begins low, then steadily increases beneath the dialogue)

The officers continue chatting pleasantly, smiling. THE NARRATOR talks more freely, voice rising, then vehemently. He paces with heavy strides, gesticulating wildly. The sound grows louder, louder, louder.

NARRATOR
(voice rising)
The old man is away. Quite secure. Nothing wrong here at all.

He swings the chair he had been sitting on, grating its legs violently upon the boards. The heartbeat thunders over everything.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
Oh God! what could I do? I foamed -- I raved -- I swore! But the noise arose over all and continually increased. It grew louder -- louder -- louder! And still the men chatted pleasantly, and smiled. Was it possible they heard not? Almighty God! -- no, no! They heard! -- they suspected! -- they knew! -- they were making a mockery of my horror!

THE NARRATOR can endure no more. His eyes bulge.

NARRATOR
(shrieking)
Villains! Dissemble no more! I admit the deed! -- tear up the planks! -- here, here! -- It is the beating of his hideous heart!

(SOUND: heartbeat at deafening volume, then abrupt silence as officers rise)

The officers seize him. The planks are torn up, revealing the dismembered remains.

INT. ASYLUM CELL - NIGHT

The same sparse stone cell. THE NARRATOR still sits facing us, now breathing hard, eyes wilder than before. Chains rattle as he leans forward.

(SOUND: single final heartbeat thump, then complete silence)

NARRATOR
(quiet, final)
Yet why will you say that I am mad?

(MUSIC: single high violin note holds, then cuts off)

FADE OUT.

THE END.
